From: slwyts <slwyts@foxmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] backlight: do not dim content when hardware access is denied

A real backlight's current brightness is already applied by the display.
Failure to open its sysfs control does not mean that brightness needs to be
emulated again in the color LUT. Steam can still control it through its
privileged writer.

On MSM, the extra gain only affected Vulkan composition: moving the pointer
darkened the display, while keyboard navigation restored direct scanout and
the original brightness. Reserve software dimming for devices without a
hardware backlight, and keep that fallback on the composition path.

--- a/src/backlight.cpp
+++ b/src/backlight.cpp
@@ -319,7 +319,11 @@
 	float GetSoftwareBacklightGain()
 	{
 		std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock( s_Mutex );
-		if ( s_bHardwareOk )
+		// A detected hardware backlight already applies its current level.
+		// Permission or write failures do not justify multiplying that level
+		// into the pixels again; Steam's privileged writer can own the control.
+		// Software dimming is only a fallback when there is no such device.
+		if ( s_bHardwareOk || s_nMax > 0 )
 			return 1.0f;
 
 		// Perceptual-ish curve so 50% on the slider is not a black image.
--- a/src/Backends/DRMBackend.cpp
+++ b/src/Backends/DRMBackend.cpp
@@ -4650,6 +4650,9 @@
 			bNeedsFullComposite |= pFrameInfo->bFadingOut;
 			bNeedsFullComposite |= !g_reshade_effect.empty();
 			bNeedsFullComposite |= steamcompmgr_has_night_rgb_ctm();
+			// Direct scanout cannot apply the software-backlight LUT.
+			bNeedsFullComposite |= pFrameInfo->applyOutputColorMgmt &&
+				g_ColorMgmt.current.flSoftwareBacklightGain != 1.0f;
 
 			if ( g_bOutputHDREnabled )
 			{
